EXPERIMENT 1
Version Control and Repository Setup with Git and GitHub

Aim:
To use version control and repository setup with Git and GitHub. 

Procedure
Download Git from the Git download webpage by selecting Download for Windows.
Select Git for Windows/x64 Setup and download the installer.
Open the downloaded installer from the Downloads folder.
Install Git by following the setup wizard.
After installation, launch Git Bash.
Download and install GitHub Desktop.
Log in to the GitHub account.
Create a new repository by selecting New Repository.
Enter the repository name and create the repository.
Open Git Bash from Windows Terminal.
Create a project folder using mkdir and enter it using cd.
Initialize Git using git init.
Configure the Git username and email.
Connect the local repository with the GitHub repository.
Create a file in the project folder.
Add the file using git add.
Commit the changes using git commit -m.
Change the branch name to main using git branch -M main.
Push the changes to GitHub using git push.
Verify the repository and file on GitHub.

These steps follow the sequence in your record. 

Result:
Thus, the Git and GitHub repository was set up successfully and the output was verified. 

For writing: You don't need to write a Java program here. It's a Git/GitHub procedure.

EXPERIMENT 2
Collaborating on GitHub Using Branches and Pull Requests

Aim:
To collaborate on GitHub using branches and pull requests. 

Procedure
Open GitHub Desktop.
Select the repository and choose Clone Repository.
Clone the repository to the local system.
Open the repository in VS Code.
Create a sample file.
Commit the file with a suitable description.
Publish the main branch.
Select Current Branch → New Branch.
Enter a branch name and create the branch.
Publish the new branch to GitHub.
Select the branch menu and choose Create Pull Request.
Enter the pull request title and description.
Push the changes to the remote repository.
Create the pull request.
Review the changes and merge the sub-branch into the main branch.
Confirm the merge.

This is essentially the exact workflow in your record. 

Result:
Thus, collaboration using branches and pull requests was successfully completed. 

Remember the story

Main → New Branch → Make Change → Commit → Push → Pull Request → Merge

That's enough to remember the whole experiment.

EXPERIMENT 3
Managing Issues and Project Boards on GitHub

Aim:
To manage issues and project boards on GitHub. 

Procedure
Open GitHub Desktop and select the repository.
Select Repository → View on GitHub.
Open the Issues tab.
Select New Issue.
Open the Projects tab.
Create a new project.
Select the Board category.
Click Create Project.
Enter a suitable project name and description.
Open the Issues section.
Create a new issue.
Select Blank Issue and click Next.
Enter the issue title and description.
Select the Projects option.
Add the issue to the created project.
Open the project board.
Move the issue through the columns:
To Do → In Progress → Done.

This matches your record's procedure. 

Result:
Thus, the issue and project board were managed successfully. 

Remember the story

Create Project → Create Issue → Add Issue to Board → To Do → In Progress → Done

That's it.

